            "title": "U.S.-China Trade Tensions Intensify, Despite Visit From Top Chinese ...",
            "snippets": [
              "Top economic aide Liu He is in the U.S. this week to discuss U.S.-China trade relations with the Trump administration. Will he be successful in reducing rising trade tensions between the two nations?\nAsia",
              "# U.S.-China Trade Tensions Intensify, Despite Visit From Top Chinese Aid To Ease Strain\nLiu He at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, on Wednesday, Jan. 24, 2018. Liu is... [+] currently in the U.S. to discuss trade.",
              "One of China's top economic aides Liu He is in the U.S. this week to discuss trade cooperation between China and the United States. He is the director of China's Office of the Central Leading Group for Financial and Economic Affairs. Liu will ascertain what the Trump administration is looking for from China, as trade relations between the two countries become increasingly strained. It seems unlikely, however, that Liu will be successful in making great strides with U.S. officials due to the negative attitude the Trump administration holds toward China",
              "Liu is meeting with U.S. Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer, Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin and economic advisor Gary Cohn to talk about bilateral trade and economic relations. Chinese State Councilor Yang Jiechi met with U.S. Secretary of State Rex Tillerson last month to discuss similar issues. The visit resulted in the announcement that both countries would resume talks under the Comprehensive Economic Dialogue, which ended in an impasse last year.",
              "The situation has already worsened even with Liu in negotiations. Just as Liu began his trip in the United States, the U.S. Commerce Department decided that aluminum foil from China was benefiting unfairly from government subsidies. The U.S. will impose anti-dumping duties on China as a result. Chinese officials were unhappy with this action, stating that the unilateral retaliatory measure contradicts WTO rules. By contrast, just this week, China has removed import duties on U.S",
              "It seems unlikely that Liu will be able to make great strides with the Trump administration. The fact that the Commerce Department decided to impose duties on Chinese aluminum foil the first day that Liu was in the U.S. underscores the fact that the Trump administration doesn’t take his visit seriously and is content with unilateral actions",
              "Although we have yet to hear of the final results of Liu's trade talks with U.S. officials, expectations for this trip are low. Perhaps the best outcome would be for China to better understand what the U.S. wants from the Asian nation trade-wise, and to continue a free and open dialogue between the two countries."
            ]
